    "created_at": "2018-07-16T12:08:29Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-07-16T12:08:29Z",
    "body": "@devsnek, there is that, though I don't know if that is enough of a risk (or impact) to reject the idea. On the other side of that, it can help support parallel programming, and task-based/threaded async.\r\n\r\nIt can also make code more reusable, with a smaller footprint. Similar functionality can be placed in a single unit, and specific variants conditionally instantiated. A case I have is selecting protocols. My worker has a specific function, but it can handle many message formats and protocols. With parameters, I can tell it the format I am using, and only that handler is instantiated. Without them, I need to specify the format with each message (and also have essentially dead code taking up memory), or write a version for each format and protocol.\r\n\r\nSomething to consider is that it's not like adding parameters to a parameterless constructor. The object bag is there already, and Chrome is actively working to expose `name`. While the spec says`name` is \"mainly useful for debugging purposes\", once it is exposed it becomes the only way to pass parameters in the constructor. It will likely be abused for this purpose (at least by me :)).",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
